        "## Exercise 1\n",
        "### 1a: Variable selection and sample restriction\n",
        "First, we remove observations before year 2000. We then select ten stock-level characteristics based on the variable-importance rankings reported in @Gu.2020, Figure 5. Figure 5 shows that characteristics related to price trends, liquidity, size, and volatility consistently rank among the most important predictors across a wide range of machine learning models. Therefore, we include the price-trend variables `mom1m` and `mom12m`, the size and liquidity measures `mvel1` and `turn`, and the risk and volatility measures `beta` and `retvol`. In addition, we include the value characteristics `bm` and `ep`, the profitability measure `roaq`, and the investment measure `invest`, which receive relatively high importance rankings in Figure 5 and represent distinct return-predictive channels documented in the asset pricing literature. Rather than selecting only the highest-ranked characteristics, we aim to capture multiple dimensions of return predictability, including momentum, size, liquidity, risk, value, profitability, and investment. Together, these variables provide exposure to several of the most prominent return-predictive characteristics identified in both the machine learning and asset pricing literature.\n",
        "\n",
        "For the macroeconomic state variables from @Goyal.2008 library, we follow @Gu.2020 Table 4. The table shows that aggregate book-to-market `bm`, the Treasury bill rate `tbl`, the default yield spread `dfy`, and the term spread `tms` are among the most influential macroeconomic variables across several machine learning specifications. We therefore select `bm`, `tbl`, `dfy`, and `tms` as our four macroeconomic predictors. These variables capture aggregate valuation conditions, short-term interest rates, credit market conditions, and the slope of the yield curve, respectively, and are widely used in empirical asset pricing as indicators of the overall economic environment."
      ],
      "id": "c1f8adaa"
    },
