        "rf_return_sign = \"positive\" if rf_test[\"ann_mean\"] > 0 else \"negative\"\n",
        "\n",
        "en_alpha_sign = \"positive\" if en_test[\"alpha\"] > 0 else \"negative\"\n",
        "rf_alpha_sign = \"positive\" if rf_test[\"alpha\"] > 0 else \"negative\"\n",
        "\n",
        "turnover_relation = \"lower\" if en_turnover[\"Test\"] < rf_turnover[\"Test\"] else \"higher\""
      ],
      "id": "8726fab9",
      "execution_count": null,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "For the selected hyperparameter specifications, `{python} preferred_model` is preferred from an investor perspective based on test-period risk-adjusted performance. In the test period, Elastic Net generates a `{python} en_return_sign` annualized mean return of `{python} f\"{en_test['ann_mean']*100:.2f}\"`%, a Sharpe ratio of `{python} f\"{en_test['sharpe']:.2f}\"`, and a `{python} en_alpha_sign` CAPM alpha of `{python} f\"{en_test['alpha']*100:.2f}\"`%. In comparison, Random Forest generates a `{python} rf_return_sign` annualized mean return of `{python} f\"{rf_test['ann_mean']*100:.2f}\"`%, a Sharpe ratio of `{python} f\"{rf_test['sharpe']:.2f}\"`, and a `{python} rf_alpha_sign` CAPM alpha of `{python} f\"{rf_test['alpha']*100:.2f}\"`%.\n",
        "\n",
        "@tbl-turnover shows that Elastic Net has `{python} turnover_relation` turnover than Random Forest in the test period. Elastic Net turnover is `{python} f\"{en_turnover['Test']:.3f}\"`, compared with `{python} f\"{rf_turnover['Test']:.3f}\"` for Random Forest. This matters because high turnover increases transaction costs and can erode gross portfolio returns.\n",
        "\n",
        "Overall, Elastic Net provides the better trade-off between risk-adjusted performance, alpha, and implementability in our sample.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n",
        "## Exercise 4\n",
        "### Exercise 4a: Variable importance\n",
        "\n",
        "Variable importance is measured as the drop in annualized test Sharpe ratio when one predictor is set to a reference value and the long-short portfolio is rebuilt. For standardized continuous predictors, the reference value is zero, corresponding to the training-sample mean after standardization. For industry dummies, the reference value is set to the test-sample mean."
      ],
      "id": "e9944bdc"
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "metadata": {
        "cache": true
      },
      "source": [
        "# baseline test Sharpe ratios from exercise 3\n",
        "baseline_sharpe_en = ls_test_en[\"ls_ret\"].mean() / ls_test_en[\"ls_ret\"].std() * np.sqrt(12)\n",
        "baseline_sharpe_rf = ls_test_rf[\"ls_ret\"].mean() / ls_test_rf[\"ls_ret\"].std() * np.sqrt(12)\n",
        "\n",
        "# reference values:\n",
        "# standardized continuous predictors -> 0\n",
        "# industry dummies -> mean in the test set\n",
        "reference_values = {\n",
        "    col: 0.0 if col in continuous_features else X_test[col].mean()\n",
        "    for col in X_test.columns\n",
        "}\n",
        "\n",
        "def variable_importance_sharpe_drop(\n",
        "    model,\n",
        "    X_test,\n",
        "    meta_test,\n",
        "    pred_col,\n",
        "    decile_col,\n",
        "    baseline_sharpe,\n",
        "    reference_values\n",
        "):\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    Measure variable importance as the drop in annualized test Sharpe ratio\n",
        "    when one predictor is set to a reference value and the portfolio is rebuilt.\n",
        "    \"\"\"\n",
        "    results = []\n",
        "\n",
        "    for feature in X_test.columns:\n",
        "        X_mod = X_test.copy()\n",
        "        X_mod[feature] = reference_values[feature]\n",
        "\n",
        "        tmp = meta_test.copy()\n",
        "        tmp[pred_col] = model.predict(X_mod)\n",
        "        tmp = assign_deciles(tmp, pred_col, decile_col)\n",
        "\n",
        "        ls_tmp = compute_longshort(tmp, decile_col)\n",
        "        perturbed_sharpe = ls_tmp[\"ls_ret\"].mean() / ls_tmp[\"ls_ret\"].std() * np.sqrt(12)\n",
        "\n",
        "        results.append({\n",
        "            \"feature\": feature,\n",
        "            \"sharpe_drop\": baseline_sharpe - perturbed_sharpe\n",
        "        })\n",
        "\n",
        "    return (\n",
        "        pd.DataFrame(results)\n",
        "        .sort_values(\"sharpe_drop\", ascending=False)\n",
        "        .reset_index(drop=True)\n",
